    42	## Divert boundary and filesystem behavior
    43	
    44	### D1 — BLOCKER: arbitrary post-seal bytes are accepted as `CLEAN_SEALED`
    45	
    46	**Lines:** `seal.py:227-255, 411-419`; `lane.sh:419-428`.
    47	
    48	`verify_bytes` looks for one matching bytes/hash/basis declaration but never
    49	requires the post-marker bytes to equal the canonical seal and never rejects
    50	unmatched prefix or suffix text. Therefore a valid seal followed by, for
    51	example, `503 upstream timeout\n` verifies; `divert_path` returns
    52	`CLEAN_SEALED`, creates neither raw nor overflow artifact, and `lane.sh` writes
    53	`report_state=BODY_SEALED`. I confirmed this behavior directly against the
    54	frozen module. This defeats the stated purpose of banking transient provider
    55	errors.
    56	
    57	**Minimal fix:** after extracting the basis, compare the complete tail against
    58	one explicitly supported canonical serialization (with a deliberate CRLF
    59	policy); any extra byte must take the `DIVERTED` path. Add a sealed-plus-suffix
    60	test. `test_seal.py:281-289` covers only a seal with no suffix—and its helper's
    61	free-form “Body definition” demonstrates that the alleged canonical form is
    62	not currently enforced.
    63	
    64	### D2 — MAJOR: an unterminated EOF marker produces contradictory custody state
    65	
    66	**Lines:** `seal.py:200-217, 395-410`; `lane.sh:425-428`.
    67	
    68	`divert` calls marker detection with `require_newline=False`, so a report ending
    69	in the marker bytes with no newline is `CLEAN` and becomes `BODY_SEALED`.
    70	The same file is rejected by `verify`/`stamp`, whose body definition requires
    71	the marker's terminating newline. Thus “sealed” is not a stable predicate even
    72	within this tool.
    73	
    74	**Minimal fix:** require LF or CRLF in `divert`, or return a distinct
    75	`UNTERMINATED_MARKER` contract failure and map it to rc 7. The verify test at
    76	`test_seal.py:87-97` rejects this input, but no `DivertTest` exercises it, so it
    77	would not catch the contradictory `report_state`.
    78	
    79	### D3 — MAJOR: diversion is not failure-atomic or durably committed
    80	
    81	**Lines:** `seal.py:357-378, 428-466`; `test_seal.py:331-339`.
    82	
    83	Final sidecar names are created and written directly. Raw is published first,
    84	then overflow, then the report is atomically replaced. If overflow already
    85	exists, the command returns failure but leaves a newly created raw file. The
    86	existing test constructs exactly that case yet omits
    87	`assertFalse(raw.exists())`. Disk-full or SIGKILL can leave a read-only,
    88	partially written final sidecar; a kill after report replacement leaves a
    89	truncated report and a still-`RUNNING` receipt. Directory-fsync errors are also
    90	silently discarded at lines 373-375 and 463-465. A same-tag retry is then
    91	refused by `lane.sh:101-107`.
    92	
    93	There is also a data-loss race at `seal.py:452-456`: bytes appended after the
    94	last comparison but before replacement are in neither the saved raw bytes nor
    95	overflow and are discarded. Early `CLEAN`/`CLEAN_SEALED` returns similarly do
    96	not revalidate the path after classification.
    97	
    98	**Minimal fix:** require writer quiescence/cooperative locking, stage and fsync
    99	all artifacts under temporary names, publish a transaction manifest last, and
   100	make recovery idempotent. Clean artifacts created by any pre-commit failure;
   101	surface directory-sync failure as a typed non-success as `stamp` already does.
